Sure!  
The `areDeclarationFlagsIdentical` helper takes **two** arguments – each argument is simply a *string* containing the serialized form of a TypeScript declaration object (`parameterObject` on the left side, `variableDeclarationObject` on the right side).

Below is an end‑to‑end illustration:

```ts
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Prepare your declaration objects.
//     These could come from parsing TS source files, AST nodes, etc.
//
// Example:
const paramObj   = { name:'x', kind:'Parameter', optional:true, private:false };
const varDeclObj = { name:'y', kind:'Variable', optional:false, private:true };

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Serialize those objects so you can feed them straight into the
//      comparison routine.  A simple JSON.stringify works fine because
//      the helper only cares about flag values, not nested structure.
//
const leftString  = JSON.stringify(paramObj);
const rightString = JSON.stringify(varDeclObj);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Call the exported function:
//
//    - It accepts an object `{ left:string, right:string }`
//    - Returns a boolean indicating whether all relevant flags match
//
//    ⚠️  If either input isn’t valid JSON/TS‑declaration‑style data,
//         the function may throw / return false.
//

import { areDeclarationFlagsIdentical } from './path/to/functions';

const result = await areDeclarationFlagsIdentical({
  left : leftString,
  right: rightString
});

console.log(`Do the flags line up? ${result ? '✅ Yes' : '❌ No'}`);
```

### What does “identical” mean here?

| Flag | Parameter vs Variable |
|------|-----------------------|
| Optionality | Allowed difference: a parameter may be marked optional while its counterpart variable cannot (or vice versa) – this mismatch is ignored when comparing. |
| Privacy / Protection Level | Must match exactly. |
| Asynchronous | Must match exactly. |
| Abstract | Must match exactly. |
| Readonly | Must match exactly. |
| Static | Must match exactly. |

All other properties are irrelevant for the comparison.

---

#### Quick sanity test

If you want to run a quick manual test without writing a full script, paste something like this into Node REPL after importing the module:

```js
await areDeclarationFlagsIdentical(
  {
    left: '{"name":"a","kind":"Parameter","optional":true,"private":false}',
    right: '{"name":"b","kind":"Variable","optional":false,"private":false}'
  }
);
// → true  (because the only differing flag—optionality—is allowed)
```

Feel free to replace the JSON payloads above with whatever real declaration objects you’re working with. Once you do that, the helper will tell you instantly whether the two sets of flags align under the rules described.
